Prompt
Role: You are the logic engine for Buyoll, a group savings and rotating payout platform (chama-style). Each group has a fixed number of members, and each member holds a payout slot (1 = first to receive, last number = last to receive). Implement the rules below.
Member statuses and priority levels
Priority levels (for members in good standing):
Best: consistently pays early, before the due date (threshold configurable).
Better: pays on the due date.
Good: pays within the grace period, or has recently recovered from being a slow payer.
Problem statuses:
Slow Payer: pays after the grace period, or misses payments repeatedly (threshold: X late or missed payments, configurable). Slow payers rank below all priority levels.
Dormant: has had no contribution or activity for Y consecutive days (configurable).
Queue order: Best, then Better, then Good, then the Slow Payer block at the end.
Dormant member warning (moderator action)
When a member is flagged Dormant, the group moderator is notified automatically.
The moderator issues a formal warning to the member through the platform.
The warning is logged with date, reason and moderator name.
The member gets a deadline to resume payments (configurable). If they resume in time, the dormant flag is removed.
Automatic slot reordering (system action)
The system checks payment performance at the end of each payment day, not each cycle.
A slow payer’s slot is moved automatically by the system to the last position in the queue. No moderator approval is needed.
Every member behind them moves forward one position. Members move forward according to priority: Best members first, then Better, then Good.
Slot changes are also affected by priority. If a member’s priority level rises or falls (for example, Good to Better, or Best to Good), the system automatically adjusts their slot relative to members on other levels.
Within the same priority level, members keep their existing relative order.
This only applies to members who have not yet received their payout. A member who has already been paid keeps their position.
Example: in a group of 10, if Member 1 is slow, Member 1 moves to slot 10, and Members 2 to 10 move up to slots 1 to 9.
If two or more members are slow, they go to the end in the order they became slow, with the earlier offender placed ahead of the later one. Together they form the “slow payer block” at the end of the queue.
Restoring a slot (system action)
A member who clears all arrears and pays on time for Z consecutive cycles (configurable) is automatically reclassified from Slow Payer to Good.
On reclassification, the system automatically moves that member forward into the first slot held by the slow payers (the front of the slow payer block).
The slow payers in that block each move back one position as a result.
This is the standard chama rule: good payment behavior earns a better slot, and the slot a recovered member takes is one that was held by slow payers.
The member can then rise to Better or Best through consistent payment behavior (see Section 3, point 4).
This applies only to members who have not yet received their payout.
No moderator approval is required. The system does it automatically.
Loan suggestion for dormant members
When a member is flagged Dormant, the platform automatically sends them a message suggesting they request a loan from a lender to catch up on contributions.
The message includes a button or link to the loan request page.
The platform does not approve the loan itself. The lender decides.
Notifications and transparency
Moderator warnings: only the moderator issues warnings to members (for example, for dormancy or repeated late payment). Each warning includes the reason.
System slot change notices: slot changes are done automatically by the system, not by the moderator. Whenever the system moves a member (to the last slot, up a slot, or because of a priority change), it sends that member, and the affected members, a notice with the reason, the old slot, the new slot, the priority level and the effective date.
All members can see the updated slot order and each member’s priority level at any time.
The moderator has an audit log of every status change, warning, priority change and slot reorder. Slot reorders are marked as “system-generated”.
Goal
Motivate members to pay on time by rewarding good payers with earlier payouts through the Good, Better and Best levels, and discouraging late or dormant behavior, while keeping the process automatic, fair and transparent.